The 'components/hud' folder holds the shared theme-shell contract (LayoutShellProps.ts's HudShellBaseProps) plus every registered theme's shell component(s) — DefaultThemeShell (an adapter around the classic LayoutShell, used at every width for the 'default' theme) and, for 'slate-amber' (originally the compact-HUD-layout plan's own component, docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-14-custom-hud-layout-design.md), a WIDTH-SPLIT pair: CompactLayoutShell (desktop, ≥901px) and CompactLayoutShellNarrow (≤900px, its own single-column layout — not a fallback to DefaultThemeShell). All conform to the same HudShellBaseProps interface so features/hudLayout/themeRegistry.ts's THEME_REGISTRY can lazy-load any of them uniformly; MainContainer.tsx calls resolveActiveTheme(themeId, viewportWidth) every render and picks whichever ShellComponent/NarrowShellComponent it resolves to, rather than choosing directly. Both slate-amber shells compose the vitals/room rows, command input, and plugin-fillable widget slots (CompactWidgetSlot, backed by features/hudLayout/hudWidgetRegistry.ts and populated by plugins via PluginRuntimeApi.setHudWidget) — the narrow one tabs Chat/Affects instead of showing both side by side, since there's no room for two columns. Styling lives in styles/hud/ (CSS modules) plus a set of plain `sa-hud-*` global classes rendered alongside each module class, which an opt-in theme (styles/hud/themes/slateAmber.theme.scss, a global side-effect import gated behind `:root[data-hud-theme='slate-amber']`, imported directly by BOTH slate-amber shells) targets instead of depending on Vite's hashed module class names.
